{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014Jjg19giP6FUcXeAa2gfxn","type":"tool_result","content":"470\t# `caffeinate python3 x.py`, `script -q /dev/null python3 x.py` — every one\n471\t# printed the dummy's output.\n472\t#\n473\t# The PRIMARY rail is now structural and needs no names at all: `_e4_scan`\n474\t# keeps testing every remaining token of a segment for INTERPRETER family, so\n475\t# `<anything> python3 <toggle>` is denied whether or not `<anything>` is\n476\t# listed here. See the INTERPRETER HOP note in the walk.\n477\t#\n478\t# What the list still buys — and why it is extended rather than deleted — is\n479\t# the DIRECT-EXEC position: `xcrun ./<toggle> on` names no interpreter, and\n480\t# the toggle ships mode 100755, so it runs. Distinguishing that from\n481\t# `cat <toggle>` is structurally impossible (the shell executes the FIRST word\n482\t# in both; only the launcher's own semantics differ), so covering it requires\n483\t# naming launchers. Each addition below is justified from THIS machine's man\n484\t# page and confirmed by running it; launchers whose binaries are absent here\n485\t# (`unbuffer`, `proxychains4`, `taskset`, `firejail`, `parallel`, `flock`,\n486\t# `torify`, `systemd-run`, `strace`, …) are deliberately NOT added from\n487\t# memory — an invented flag table false-positives on readers, and the\n488\t# structural rail already covers their interpreter form.\n489\t_E4_PREFIX_RUNNERS = frozenset({\n490\t    \"exec\", \"command\", \"nohup\", \"env\", \"sudo\", \"doas\", \"nocorrect\",\n491\t    \"setsid\", \"stdbuf\", \"xargs\", \"time\", \"timeout\", \"nice\", \"ionice\",\n492\t    # macOS launchers, measured present + executing on this platform.\n493\t    \"xcrun\", \"arch\", \"caffeinate\", \"script\", \"sandbox-exec\",\n494\t})\n495\t_E4_DURATION_RE = re.compile(r\"^[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?[smhd]?$\")\n496\t\n497\t# Runner flags that consume the NEXT token as their value, so it is not\n498\t# mistaken for the script operand (`python3 -X importtime script.py`).\n499\t_E4_RUNNER_VALUE_FLAGS = frozenset({\n500\t    \"-W\", \"-X\", \"--check-hash-based-pycs\",      # python\n501\t    \"-o\", \"+o\", \"--rcfile\", \"--init-file\",      # POSIX shells\n502\t})\n503\t\n504\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n505\t# PREFIX-RUNNER flag table — codex S292 P1-B.\n506\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n507\t# The first implementation skipped any token starting with `-` and then took\n508\t# the first non-flag token as the command word. For a VALUE-BEARING flag that\n509\t# token is the flag's VALUE, not a command:\n510\t#\n511\t#     env  -u FOO  python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py on\n512\t#              ^^^ read as the command word → the rest was never examined\n513\t#     sudo -u root python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py on\n514\t#\n515\t# Both returned ALLOW. The scan must resolve the whole runner chain.\n516\t#\n517\t# DERIVATION (the S291 lesson: a closed set written from memory errs in BOTH\n518\t# directions, so each entry below names its authority):\n519\t#   env, xargs, stdbuf, nice  → this machine's man pages, `man 1 <cmd>`\n520\t#   sudo                      → this machine's `man 8 sudo` option list\n521\t#   timeout, ionice, setsid   → GNU coreutils / util-linux documented\n522\t#   doas, time                → synopsis (binaries absent on darwin, so the\n523\t#                               man page could not be read here — flagged in\n524\t#                               the NOTES as the weakest rows)\n525\t#\n526\t# BOTH error directions are covered, which is why the table carries the\n527\t# BOOLEAN flags too rather than only the value-bearing ones:\n528\t#   • a value flag recorded as unknown → the value is examined as a command\n529\t#     word AND the scan continues one token further (see `ambiguous` in the"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"952b1749-d638-4be4-822c-3ae71fe52d2b","uuid":"c7f360f4-40c4-4026-9fdc-d0b5d9226932","timestamp":"2026-11-15T10:18:24.368Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.tG8bbW/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/posture_tables.py","content":"# `caffeinate python3 x.py`, `script -q /dev/null python3 x.py` — every one\n# printed the dummy's output.\n#\n# The PRIMARY rail is now structural and needs no names at all: `_e4_scan`\n# keeps testing every remaining token of a segment for INTERPRETER family, so\n# `<anything> python3 <toggle>` is denied whether or not `<anything>` is\n# listed here. See the INTERPRETER HOP note in the walk.\n#\n# What the list still buys — and why it is extended rather than deleted — is\n# the DIRECT-EXEC position: `xcrun ./<toggle> on` names no interpreter, and\n# the toggle ships mode 100755, so it runs. Distinguishing that from\n# `cat <toggle>` is structurally impossible (the shell executes the FIRST word\n# in both; only the launcher's own semantics differ), so covering it requires\n# naming launchers. Each addition below is justified from THIS machine's man\n# page and confirmed by running it; launchers whose binaries are absent here\n# (`unbuffer`, `proxychains4`, `taskset`, `firejail`, `parallel`, `flock`,\n# `torify`, `systemd-run`, `strace`, …) are deliberately NOT added from\n# memory — an invented flag table false-positives on readers, and the\n# structural rail already covers their interpreter form.\n_E4_PREFIX_RUNNERS = frozenset({\n    \"exec\", \"command\", \"nohup\", \"env\", \"sudo\", \"doas\", \"nocorrect\",\n    \"setsid\", \"stdbuf\", \"xargs\", \"time\", \"timeout\", \"nice\", \"ionice\",\n    # macOS launchers, measured present + executing on this platform.\n    \"xcrun\", \"arch\", \"caffeinate\", \"script\", \"sandbox-exec\",\n})\n_E4_DURATION_RE = re.compile(r\"^[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?[smhd]?$\")\n\n# Runner flags that consume the NEXT token as their value, so it is not\n# mistaken for the script operand (`python3 -X importtime script.py`).\n_E4_RUNNER_VALUE_FLAGS = frozenset({\n    \"-W\", \"-X\", \"--check-hash-based-pycs\",      # python\n    \"-o\", \"+o\", \"--rcfile\", \"--init-file\",      # POSIX shells\n})\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# PREFIX-RUNNER flag table — codex S292 P1-B.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# The first implementation skipped any token starting with `-` and then took\n# the first non-flag token as the command word. For a VALUE-BEARING flag that\n# token is the flag's VALUE, not a command:\n#\n#     env  -u FOO  python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py on\n#              ^^^ read as the command word → the rest was never examined\n#     sudo -u root python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py on\n#\n# Both returned ALLOW. The scan must resolve the whole runner chain.\n#\n# DERIVATION (the S291 lesson: a closed set written from memory errs in BOTH\n# directions, so each entry below names its authority):\n#   env, xargs, stdbuf, nice  → this machine's man pages, `man 1 <cmd>`\n#   sudo                      → this machine's `man 8 sudo` option list\n#   timeout, ionice, setsid   → GNU coreutils / util-linux documented\n#   doas, time                → synopsis (binaries absent on darwin, so the\n#                               man page could not be read here — flagged in\n#                               the NOTES as the weakest rows)\n#\n# BOTH error directions are covered, which is why the table carries the\n# BOOLEAN flags too rather than only the value-bearing ones:\n#   • a value flag recorded as unknown → the value is examined as a command\n#     word AND the scan continues one token further (see `ambiguous` in the","numLines":60,"startLine":470,"totalLines":536}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014Jjg19giP6FUcXeAa2gfxn","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4YS4KZX5ufU64EFLGrP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RB1i8FwejTjWSNKkgwByeN","name":"Grep","input":{"pattern":"def _normalize_command_tokens","path":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.tG8bbW/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts","output_mode":"content","-A":60},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2607,"cache_read_input_tokens":150421,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2607},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"952b1749-d638-4be4-822c-3ae71fe52d2b","uuid":"9334c26a-72f6-4785-9fc8-d7423ce93535","timestamp":"2026-11-15T10:18:26.649Z","request_id":"req_011Ch4YS47QsCcK5up17ZdgV","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01RB1i8FwejTjWSNKkgwByeN":{"pattern":"def _normalize_command_tokens","path":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.tG8bbW/tree/.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts","output_mode":"content","-A":60}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RB1i8FwejTjWSNKkgwByeN","type":"tool_result","content":".claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py:108:def _normalize_command_tokens(tokens: List[str]) -> List[str]:\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-109-    \"\"\"Strip privilege-escalation prefixes + normalize the command token.\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-110-\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-111-    Behavior (see P0-02 above):\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-112-\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-113-    - Drops leading ``sudo`` / ``doas`` / ``nocorrect`` tokens AND the\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-114-      flags that belong to them (``-u USER``, ``--user=USER``, ``-i``,\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-115-      ``-s``, ``-k``, ``-n``, ``-E``, etc.). Heuristic: after the prefix,\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-116-      any token starting with ``-`` is consumed as a prefix-flag; a known\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-117-      value-taking flag (``-u`` / ``--user``) additionally consumes the\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-118-      following non-flag token as its argument.\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-119-    - Replaces ``tokens[0]`` with its basename (``/bin/rm`` → ``rm``,\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-120-      ``./rm`` → ``rm``) after stripping a single leading backslash\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-121-      (shell alias-escape: ``\\\\rm`` → ``rm``).\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-122-    - PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175: additionally drops leading shell env\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-123-      assignments (``NAME=VALUE``) and the ``env`` prefix runner, so\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-124-      ``FOO=1 rm -rf /`` / ``env FOO=1 rm -rf /`` / the citation channel\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-125-      ``CEO_DESTRUCTIVE_CITE='…' rm -rf /`` all reach the same matcher\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-126-      decision as a bare ``rm -rf /``.\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-127-\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-128-    Returns a NEW list; never mutates ``tokens``. Empty input returns\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-129-    the input unchanged. Pure function (no I/O).\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-130-\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-131-    Examples\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-132-    --------\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-133-    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"sudo\", \"rm\", \"-rf\", \"/\"])\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-134-    ['rm', '-rf', '/']\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-135-    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"/bin/rm\", \"-rf\", \"/tmp\"])\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-136-    ['rm', '-rf', '/tmp']\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-137-    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"\\\\\\\\rm\", \"-rf\", \"/tmp\"])\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-138-    ['rm', '-rf', '/tmp']\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-139-    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"sudo\", \"-u\", \"root\", \"rm\", \"-rf\", \"/\"])\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-140-    ['rm', '-rf', '/']\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-141-    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"doas\", \"rm\", \"-rf\", \"/\"])\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-142-    ['rm', '-rf', '/']\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-143-    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([])\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-144-    []\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-145-    \"\"\"\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-146-    if not tokens:\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-147-        return tokens\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-148-    working = list(tokens)\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-149-    # Drop leading privilege prefixes (sudo/doas/nocorrect/env) and any flags\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-150-    # that belong to them.\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-151-    # >>> PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175 citation-gate BEGIN (strip-loop restructure)\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-152-    # Interleaved fixed-point loop: leading NAME=VALUE env assignments and\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-153-    # privilege/env prefixes may alternate (`FOO=1 sudo rm …`,\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-154-    # `sudo env FOO=1 rm …`, `CEO_DESTRUCTIVE_CITE='…' rm …`) — keep\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-155-    # stripping until the first real command token. Pure; never raises.\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-156-    while working:\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-157-        if _ENV_ASSIGNMENT_RE.match(working[0]):\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-158-            working.pop(0)  # leading shell env assignment (NAME=VALUE)\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-159-            continue\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-160-        if working[0] in _PRIVILEGE_PREFIXES:\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-161-            working.pop(0)\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-162-            # Consume prefix-owned flags: any -flag until we hit a non-flag\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-163-            # or run out. For value-taking flags (-u USER / --user=USER /\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-164-            # env's -C DIR / --chdir DIR / --unset NAME), also pop the\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-165-            # following non-flag value token.\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-166-            while working and working[0].startswith(\"-\"):\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-167-                flag = working.pop(0)\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-168-                if (flag in _PREFIX_VALUE_FLAGS"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"952b1749-d638-4be4-822c-3ae71fe52d2b","uuid":"c38b32ab-9a39-4a10-952b-84e467430b43","timestamp":"2026-11-15T10:18:26.755Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":".claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py:108:def _normalize_command_tokens(tokens: List[str]) -> List[str]:\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-109-    \"\"\"Strip privilege-escalation prefixes + normalize the command token.\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-110-\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-111-    Behavior (see P0-02 above):\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-112-\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-113-    - Drops leading ``sudo`` / ``doas`` / ``nocorrect`` tokens AND the\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-114-      flags that belong to them (``-u USER``, ``--user=USER``, ``-i``,\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-115-      ``-s``, ``-k``, ``-n``, ``-E``, etc.). Heuristic: after the prefix,\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-116-      any token starting with ``-`` is consumed as a prefix-flag; a known\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-117-      value-taking flag (``-u`` / ``--user``) additionally consumes the\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-118-      following non-flag token as its argument.\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-119-    - Replaces ``tokens[0]`` with its basename (``/bin/rm`` → ``rm``,\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-120-      ``./rm`` → ``rm``) after stripping a single leading backslash\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-121-      (shell alias-escape: ``\\\\rm`` → ``rm``).\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-122-    - PLAN-153.E5 / ADR-175: additionally drops leading shell env\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-123-      assignments (``NAME=VALUE``) and the ``env`` prefix runner, so\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-124-      ``FOO=1 rm -rf /`` / ``env FOO=1 rm -rf /`` / the citation channel\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-125-      ``CEO_DESTRUCTIVE_CITE='…' rm -rf /`` all reach the same matcher\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-126-      decision as a bare ``rm -rf /``.\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-127-\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-128-    Returns a NEW list; never mutates ``tokens``. Empty input returns\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-129-    the input unchanged. Pure function (no I/O).\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-130-\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-131-    Examples\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-132-    --------\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-133-    >>> _normalize_command_tokens([\"sudo\", \"rm\", \"-rf\", \"/\"])\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-134-    ['rm', '-rf', '/']\n.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/normalization.py-135-    