{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4ZHMNM9S429AXEL6NjY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014Nd8mZq8ZBRukcRhSXa5DA","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"report":"PAYLOAD 16/32: what I checked\nFiles: .claude/hooks/_lib/audit_emit.py (the end of the facade rewrite), the new bash_indirect_guard.py and bash_indirect_telemetry.py, and the new canonical_edit/__init__.py and canonical_edit/decisions.py. I read every file, its callers and the related tests in the read-only tree. I did not run any code: all traces below come from reading the source.\n\nDECLARED CONDITIONS THIS PAYLOAD CAN TEST: none is false\n- Condition 5 holds. check_arbitration_kernel.py:309-340 has 13 new rows: 7 recursive package rows (canonical_edit, agent_spawn, _policy_parts, _audit_emit_parts, _audit_log_parts, _bash_safety_parts, _pair_rail_parts), 5 flat modules (model_currency, cli_currency, plan_paths, bash_indirect_guard, bash_indirect_telemetry) and adapters/live. All five files in this payload are covered.\n- Condition 6 holds. posture_scan.py:571-584 runs the scan unless the trusted value is \"0\". With no trusted snapshot, the switch defaults to ON. decisions_cli.py:168-174 sends telemetry only when a switch is \"0\".\n- Condition 7 holds. check_canonical_edit.py:618-620 binds canonical_edit, and audit_emit.py:413-452 binds the _audit_emit_parts modules.\n\nP0: none found.\n\nNEW FINDINGS (annex)\n\nP1-1. Common valid commands are blocked as \"unparseable\"\n- Where: bash_indirect_guard.py:159-171.\n- Cause: the code that finds the end of a $(...) or <(...) body ignores quotes and plain \"(\". The first \")\" ends the body.\n- Trace: V=$(python3 -c \"print(1)\") gives the body: python3 -c \"print(1. Strict _words then raises \"No closing quotation\", and lines 489-493 return bash_indirect_unparseable_shell. posture_scan.py:582 tags that block destructive=True.\n- Other blocked examples: echo \"$(node -p \"require('./package.json').version\")\" and n=$(jq '[.[]|select(.ok)]|length' f.json).\n- The block is not tied to the heuristics switch, so only CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD=0 avoids it. The allow tests (test_check_bash_safety_indirect_exec.py:66-82) have no such case.\n- Adopter impact: routine read-only one-liners get blocked in upgraded sessions. Each block can be worked around by rewriting the command. The session is not stopped.\n- Minimal fix: inside the body scan, skip quoted spans and count unquoted \"(\" as nesting (bash's own matching rule). Add the three examples as allow tests.\n\nP1-2. The scan's limits let commands through, against CHANGELOG [1.4.4] lines 93-94 (\"fails closed ... at its depth, size and word limits\")\n- (a) Where: bash_indirect_guard.py:386-399. _scan_segment recurses into xargs/find bodies with depth+1 but never checks MAX_DEPTH. Example: echo ~ | xargs xargs ... xargs rm -rf, with about 1000 xargs. That stays under MAX_WORDS (1024) and is about 6 KB, under MAX_BODY_BYTES. The recursion raises RecursionError, decisions_cli.py:243 catches it, and the hook ALLOWS. The repo's own doctrine (posture_parser.py:369) calls a RecursionError inside a security matcher a fail-open.\n- (b) Where: bash_indirect_guard.py:243. _normalise strips at most 12 launchers, then returns a launcher as the command word. \"command\" repeated 13 times, then rm -rf ~, passes this scan. It also passes the base _check_rm_rf, because normalization.py:103 does not strip command or nohup. A single \"command rm -rf ~\" is blocked.\n- Neither case is a regression against v1.4.3-rc.1, where both commands were already allowed.\n- Minimal fix: return _hit(\"bash_indirect_limit\") when depth > MAX_DEPTH at the top of _scan_segment, and when _normalise uses all 12 passes and still has a launcher as head. Catch RecursionError in scan() and return the same limit hit.\n\nP1-3. Two parsing defects let a simple prefix skip the scan\nBoth contradict CHANGELOG line 84 (\"inside sh -c, eval or a here-doc\"). Neither is a regression against the base.\n- (a) Where: bash_indirect_guard.py:95-96. Comment stripping treats \"{\", \"}\" and \")\" as the start of a new word. So the \"#\" in ${#var}, ${x}# or $(cmd)# is read as a comment, and the rest of the line is dropped before both _command_substitutions (line 144) and _words (line 101). Example: n=${#list}; sh -c 'rm -rf ~'. The scan sees only \"n=${\" and ALLOWS. The base guard also allows it, because the command word is sh.\n- (b) Where: bash_indirect_guard.py:224-227. The heredoc \"consumer\" is taken as the first word of all text since the start or the previous heredoc, not the command that owns the \"<<\". So true && bash <<'EOF' (body rm -rf ~), FOO=1 bash <<EOF, sudo sh <<EOF and cd x; bash <<EOF never have their body scanned. The opaque-stdin check (line ~523) looks for \"<\" in the token list, not \"<<\", so these commands are ALLOWED.\n- Minimal fix: treat \"{\" and \"}\" as part of a word, and a \")\" that closes \"$(\" as part of a word. Take the heredoc consumer from the last simple command in the prefix, after _normalise. Treat \"<<\" like \"<\" in the opaque-stdin check. Add tests for both.\n\nP2 follow-ups\n- P2-1. False positives of the heuristic forms (switch-gated; condition 6 says false-positive limits were never measured):\n  - bash_indirect_guard.py:400-403 matches a bare -r or -f after a computed command word, so $PY -m pip install -r requirements.txt is blocked.\n  - Line ~523 blocks bash ./x.sh < /dev/null as opaque stdin.\n  - Fix: require both r and f for rm-style flags, and exempt a shell invoked with a script operand.\n- P2-2. Glob false positive at bash_indirect_guard.py:431-435. fnmatch's \"*\" crosses \"/\", so fnmatchcase(\".claude/settings.json\", \"*.json\") is True. Running sed -i 's/a/b/' *.json (or *.toml, *.yaml) at the project root is then blocked as bash_computed_canonical_target. Fix: match the glob segment by segment.\n- P2-3. Launcher coverage. _normalise (line 249) does not strip exec, timeout, nice, setsid or stdbuf, although _E4_PREFIX_RUNNERS (posture_tables.py:489-494) lists them. exec rm -rf ~ and timeout 5 rm -rf ~ pass both rails. This gap predates the release; align the two sets.\n- P2-4. Doc nit. CHANGELOG lines 98-99 say CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS=0 turns off \"only the computed-verb and opaque-feeder forms\". It also turns off the env ambiguous-launcher form (line 357).\n- P2-5. bash_indirect_guard.py:457 treats python's -E (ignore environment) as an eval flag. python3 -E tool.py PROTOCOL.md is then blocked with a misleading \"indirect shell execution\" message.\n- P2-6. bash_indirect_telemetry.py:51-58 writes one marker file per session per switch under <audit dir>/bash-indirect-disabled/ and never prunes them.\n- P2-7. Adjacent file, reviewed in another payload: check_canonical_edit.py:616-640.\n  - If any component imported at canonical_edit/__init__.py:12 raises ImportError, every Edit/Write is blocked, canonical path or not.\n  - A non-ImportError failure, such as a SyntaxError from a truncated file, instead allows every edit, canonical paths included.\n  - ADR-202:56-59 assigns this to separate import-integrity work. The CHANGELOG should say what an adopter sees after a partial copy.\n  - In fallback mode, _cli_is_canonical is undefined, so the --is-canonical oracle raises NameError.\n- P2-8. Cosmetic issues in audit_emit.py:\n  - Lines 473-480: emit_generic has three identical branches.\n  - Lines 21 and 382-383: a pre-install iter_events delegates to the unbound template module. It is overwritten at lines 415 and 460, so it has no runtime effect.\n\nChecked and found clean\n- audit_emit facade end:\n  - 25 names removed from the facade were spot-checked, and each is exported by a part.\n  - The locks are recreated after install (lines 483-484) and are never deep-copied.\n  - _try_rate_admit's globals() resolves to the facade.\n  - _KNOWN_ACTIONS contains learning_rail_disabled.\n  - The rail and switch enums gain bash_indirect and both switch names (lifecycle_schemas.py:508-517), matching CHANGELOG lines 105-106.\n  - The spool forensic emitter is still wired.\n- Telemetry:\n  - It catches every exception, so it can never change the verdict.\n  - The marker is created with O_EXCL, so a symlink in its place is not followed.\n  - The session id is hashed before it becomes a file name, and the marker stays inside the audit family directory.\n- canonical_edit binding:\n  - Every entry in each component's __all__ is a function, and functions resolve in the hook's globals, so patch points are kept.\n  - decisions.py keeps both ADR-186 fail-CLOSED wall-deadline checks (lines 82 and 100).\n- Fail direction:\n  - The guard is imported (posture_scan.py:571) only after all base checks have passed, so no earlier block is lost, and an ImportError is allowed by decisions_cli.py:243.\n  - In _canonical_target, an ImportError returns False, i.e. fails open.\n- Python: all five files use only the standard library and are compatible with Python 3.9.","decision":"GO-WITH-CONDITIONS","sentence":"All three declared conditions this payload can test (5, 6 and 7) hold against the code and no P0 was found, but the new indirect-execution guard, on by default, blocks common valid $(...) one-liners and lets commands through at limits the CHANGELOG says fail closed, so the three P1 annex items should be fixed or declared before GA."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5113,"cache_read_input_tokens":263907,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5113},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"952b1749-d638-4be4-822c-3ae71fe52d2b","uuid":"c94aa1fc-068f-4c73-80f0-99491db3b104",